{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794239868,"nonce":"YzxilTY3ZEa/o211n3lt37JQcs4=","title":"A complex linear solve with a direct backend factorises the 2n real block, not the n complex operator","message":"fem.solve(linear=jno.solve.lu(backend='pardiso')) on a complex form hands PARDISO the fused real-equivalent block [[Ar, -Ai], [Ai, Ar]] (mtype 11, real unsymmetric, size 2n). MKL PARDISO takes complex matrices directly (mtype 13, or 6 when complex symmetric) through the same phase calls jNO already drives; pypardiso's public check refuses complex, its _call_pardiso does not.\n\nMeasured on the periodic quartz|air cell with exact faces (degree 2): the complex n x n factorisation needs about half the memory of the fused one and less analysis and factor time, at the same residual. The host work around the solve (periodic reduction of the fused matrix in _remap_bcoo_weighted, the symmetry-pattern sort in _cudss_sym_perms, the device-to-host copies) scales with the fused block's entries, four times the complex operator's, and was larger than the factorisation itself on that cell.\n\nThe complex-native route already exists for AMS (_solve_complex_block); a direct backend that takes complex matrices could use it. Differentiability (the custom_linear_solve transpose) must be kept.","files":null}]}